Theorem (The Twin-Equation Transcendental Extraction)

Let  \pi  denote the circle constant.
Let  r_1 = \frac{p_1}{q_1}  and  r_2 = \frac{p_2}{q_2}  be two distinct rational approximations of  \pi  
with known integer numerators and denominators.

Define the following twin expressions involving an unknown variable  x , the algebraic irrational  \sqrt{2} , 
and the rational constants  r_1, r_2 :

\begin{aligned}
E_1(x) &= \alpha x + \sqrt{2} + r_1 \\
E_2(x) &= \beta x - \sqrt{2} - r_2
\end{aligned}

where  \alpha  and  \beta  are fixed positive integers.

---

Condition 1 (Geometric Consistency)

If  x = \pi , then the following cancellation identities hold simultaneously:

\begin{aligned}
E_1(\pi) + E_2(\pi) &= (\alpha + \beta)\pi + (r_1 - r_2) \\
E_1(\pi) - E_2(\pi) &= (\alpha - \beta)\pi + (r_1 + r_2) + 2\sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}

---

Condition 2 (The Extraction Condition)

Choose  \alpha, \beta, r_1, r_2  such that:

r_1 + r_2 = 0 \quad \text{(constants cancel in the sum)}

Then:

E_1(\pi) + E_2(\pi) = (\alpha + \beta)\pi

---

Condition 3 (The Numerical Decoupling)

Now, construct the same expressions without assuming  x = \pi  — treat  x  as unknown. 
Evaluate  E_1(x)  and  E_2(x)  numerically using only:

· The known decimal expansion of  \sqrt{2}  (algebraic, computable to arbitrary precision)
· The rational numbers  r_1, r_2, \alpha, \beta 

Then:

x = \frac{E_1(x) + E_2(x)}{\alpha + \beta}

---

Corollary (Non-Circular Computation of π)

If we set:

\alpha = 1,\quad \beta = 2,\quad r_1 = \frac{355}{113},\quad r_2 = -\frac{355}{113}

then  r_1 + r_2 = 0 , and:

E_1(\pi) + E_2(\pi) = 3\pi

But  E_1(x) + E_2(x)  as a numeric expression (using  \sqrt{2}  and rationals only) evaluates to the same value 
as  3\pi . Therefore:

\pi = \frac{E_1(x) + E_2(x)}{3}

where the right-hand side is computed without any prior knowledge of π's decimal expansion 
— only using  \sqrt{2}  and rational arithmetic.

